      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "INTRODUCTION",
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Herpes simplex virus type 2 (HSV2) infection is the primary cause of genital herpes. It is\nhighly prevalent in human populations in many parts of the world, and is the most\ncommon cause of genital ulcer disease worldwide.\nIn developing countries, the major public health importance of HSV2 relates to its\npotential role in facilitating HIV transmission. HSV2 is highly prevalent in most regions\nexperiencing severe HIV epidemics, with infection rates rising steeply with age to reach\nlevels of 70% or more among adult women and men in some African countries. Genital\nulcer disease enhances the infect",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the workshop by setting out the public health importance of HSV2 in developing countries and its suspected role in HIV spread. It explains that experts from developing and industrialized countries were invited to consider control measures.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
