    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "OBJECTIVES",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-2-objectives.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1. To review existing knowledge in the following four areas, with a particular focus on\ndeveloping countries:\n■The epidemiology and natural history of HSV2\n■The interaction between HSV2 and HIV\n■HSV2 control measures\n■HSV2 diagnostics.\n2.To identify important gaps in knowledge in each of these areas requiring further study.\n3.To establish priorities for future research and control programmes.\n2. OBJECTIVES\n2\n2. OBJECTIVES\nHERPES SIMPLES VIRUS TYPE 2: PROGRAMMATIC AND RESEARCH PRIORITIES IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States the workshop's objectives: to review knowledge on HSV2 epidemiology, its interaction with HIV, control measures and diagnostics, to identify knowledge gaps, and to set research and programme priorities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PARTICIPANTS AND METHODOLOGY",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-3-participants-and-methodology.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Forty participants attended the workshop (Annex 1), and areas of expertise included\nvirology, epidemiology, surveillance, behavioural science, clinical medicine,\nmathematical modelling and STI programme development and implementation in\ndeveloping countries. Participants came from Africa (10),Asia (1), Europe (18) and\nNorth America (7). In addition, three representatives of UN agencies were present, and\none from GlaxoSmithKline.\nThe workshop was organised around the four topic areas listed above, with a session\ndevoted to each topic.The workshop included presentations by invited speakers, brea",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the 40 participants and the workshop's format of presentations, breakout groups and plenary prioritisation. It then reviews the epidemiology and natural history of HSV2, including rising prevalence worldwide and its role as the leading cause of genital ulcer disease. Table 1 gives seroprevalence figures for general populations in Africa, Latin America and Asia, and the section discusses HSV2 as a marker of sexual behaviour. It ends with recommended studies, including of discordant couples, with attention to counselling and treatment.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Biological and clinical research",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-51-biological-and-clinical-research.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HIV and HSV2 manifest a bi-directional interaction. HSV2 increases the efficiency of HIV\nacquisition and transmission whereas HIV may increase susceptibility to HSV2 and\nincrease HSV2 shedding, HSV2 recurrence rate and severity of clinical manifestations.\nEFFECT OF HIV INFECTION ON NATURAL HISTORY OF HSV2\nHSV2 reactivation and duration of recurrences are significantly increased in HIV infected\nindividuals [Augenbraun et al., 1995; Fennema et al., 1995].The frequency and severity of\nrecurrences increases as CD4 cell count decreases [Augenbraun et al., 1995; Fennema et\nal., 1995; Pannuti et al.,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the two-way biological interaction between HIV and HSV2, with HIV increasing HSV2 reactivation, shedding and severity, and HSV2 lesions containing HIV. It notes some evidence that aciclovir may prolong survival in people with HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "INTERACTION BETWEEN HSV2 AND HIV",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-5-interaction-between-hsv2-and-hiv.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "10\n5. INTERACTION BETWEEN HSV2 AND HIV\nHERPES SIMPLES VIRUS TYPE 2: PROGRAMMATIC AND RESEARCH PRIORITIES IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES\nassociation between genital HIV RNA and HSV2 DNA levels, but some association of\nquantity of HIV and HSV2 shedding among dually shedding women [Mbopi-Keou et al.,\n2000]. HIV RNA is often present at high titres in genital lesions (independent of plasma\nRNA levels), but titres of both HIV1 and HSV2 fall rapidly on treatment with aciclovir,\nsupporting the hypothesis that HSV2 reactivation may play an important role in \nup-regulation of HIV1 on mucosal surfaces.\nFew data",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the review of how HSV2 may increase HIV shedding, noting that aciclovir lowers both viruses in lesions and that data from developing countries are lacking. It lists recommended biological studies, such as whether the effect differs in Africa because of co-infections.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Epidemiological research",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-52-epidemiological-research.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "EFFECT OF HSV2 ON HIV ACQUISITION\nEvidence for the effect of HSV2 on acquisition of HIV comes from epidemiological\nstudies, and results of a meta-analysis of the relationships between the two infections\nwere presented [Wald and Link, unpublished]. Nine prospective studies (four cohort\n5. INTERACTION BETWEEN HSV2 AND HIV\n11\nHERPES SIMPLES VIRUS TYPE 2: PROGRAMMATIC AND RESEARCH PRIORITIES IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES\nstudies and five nested case-control studies) were identified. Studies in men showed a\nconsistent two-fold increase in the risk of HIV acquisition. In contrast, the study in\nwomen did n",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises epidemiological evidence that HSV2 raises the risk of acquiring HIV, from a meta-analysis showing about a two-fold risk in men and weaker findings in women. It discusses why cross-sectional studies show stronger associations and recommends prospective studies and estimates of population attributable fractions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-42458/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Genital ulcer disease in Africa: Epidemiological trends",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-53-genital-ulcer-disease-in-africa-epidemiologic.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Chancroid, syphilis and genital herpes are all common causes of genital ulcer disease\n(GUD) in developing countries. However, there is some evidence that there have been\nchanges in the aetiology of GUD in recent years [O’Farrell, 1999], and this may\ninfluence the appropriateness of current syndromic management.\nTable 2 shows data from a gold mine STI clinic in South Africa from 1986 to 1998 [Htun\net al., 2001].The proportions of GUD due to chancroid and syphilis decreased during the\nstudy period. Of the 239 GUD diagnosed in 1986, 53% were chancroid and 12%\nsyphilis. By 1998, 33% and 3% of 200",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes changes in the causes of genital ulcer disease in Africa, with Table 2 showing herpes rising and chancroid and syphilis falling at a South African mine clinic from 1986 to 1998. It notes that this undermines syndromic management and recommends monitoring ulcer causes by sex, HIV status and risk group.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
